Subject: [PATCH] kernel: make socket(SOCK_NONBLOCK) actually non-blocking

create_socket_fd() turned SOCK_NONBLOCK into O_NONBLOCK on the file
descriptor's open mode and nothing else. The network stack decides whether
connect(), recv() and friends block, and it never looks at the open mode, so
the socket stayed blocking while F_GETFL said O_NONBLOCK. fcntl(F_SETFL,
O_NONBLOCK) did not have the problem: socket_set_flags() sends the stack
B_SET_NONBLOCKING_IO.

Found through curl, which creates its sockets with SOCK_NONBLOCK and trusts
F_GETFL: its recv() before closing a kept-alive connection waited for the
server's idle timeout, 30 to 400 seconds on every run. strace showed it:

  socket(AF_INET, SOCK_STREAM|SOCK_NONBLOCK|SOCK_CLOEXEC, 0x6) = 0x4
  connect(0x4, ...) = 0x0 No error (33310 us)         <- blocked
  fcntl(0x4, F_GETFL) = 0xc2                          <- says O_NONBLOCK
  recv(0x4, ..., 0x400, 0) = 0x0 (400021034 us)

Reproduced on x86 (Sony VAIO P) and arm64 alike: a connect() to an
unreachable address on a SOCK_NONBLOCK socket took 75 s instead of returning
EINPROGRESS. With this change it returns at once, verified on an image built
from this tree. accept4() and socketpair() go through the same function.

diff -ru a/src/system/kernel/fs/socket.cpp b/src/system/kernel/fs/socket.cpp
--- a/src/system/kernel/fs/socket.cpp	2026-09-30 09:39:31.143890407 +0000
+++ b/src/system/kernel/fs/socket.cpp	2026-09-30 09:39:31.144890692 +0000
@@ -394,6 +394,19 @@
 		SO_NONBLOCK, &nonBlock, &nonBlockLen);
 	if (error != B_OK)
 		return error;
+	// SOCK_NONBLOCK has to reach the socket, not just the descriptor's open
+	// mode: the stack decides whether connect(), recv() and friends block,
+	// and it never looks at the open mode. Setting only O_NONBLOCK left a
+	// socket that F_GETFL reported non-blocking but that blocked anyway, so
+	// callers that trust it (curl) sat in recv() until the peer closed.
+	// fcntl(F_SETFL, O_NONBLOCK) goes through socket_set_flags(), which does
+	// the same.
+	if ((flags & SOCK_NONBLOCK) != 0 && !nonBlock) {
+		error = sStackInterface->ioctl(socket, B_SET_NONBLOCKING_IO, NULL, 0);
+		if (error != B_OK)
+			return error;
+	}
+
 	int oflags = 0;
 	if ((flags & SOCK_CLOEXEC) != 0)
 		oflags |= O_CLOEXEC;
